<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">D. S. Senanayake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Don Stephen Senanayake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">D. S. Senanayake was a Sri Lankan politician who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> to independence and served as its first prime minister from 1947 until his death in 1952. A founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-party">United National Party</a>, he promoted irrigation and peasant settlement in the dry zone. He died after a fall from his horse and was succeeded by his son Dudley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/d-s-senanayake.jpg" alt="D. S. Senanayake" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image is a work of an employee of the Government of Ceylon (Sri Lanka) / Scanned Edited &amp; Uploaded By MediaJet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Photographic_Portrait_of_Don_Stephen_Senanayaka_(1884-1952).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Don Stephen Senanayake was born in the village of Botale in the Hapitigam Korale, now Mirigama, the son of Don Spater Senanayake, who had made a fortune in graphite mining and was expanding into plantations and the arrack renting franchise. He was brought up in a devout Buddhist family and attended the Anglican school S. Thomas' College, Mutwal, where he excelled at cricket rather than study. After a spell as a clerk in the Surveyor General's Department he joined his brother D.</p><p>C. Senanayake in running the family's business holdings, introducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> to the plantations and managing the Kahatagaha Graphite Mine. The three Senanayake brothers were active in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temperance-movement">temperance movement</a> formed in 1912, and during the 1915 riots they were imprisoned without charges; Don Stephen was released on bail after 46 days in Welikada Prison. The heavy-handed suppression of the riots by the British colonial authorities began the modern independence movement led by the educated middle class.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Senanayake was elected unopposed to the Legislative Council in 1924 and, after his brother F. R. Senanayake died in 1925, took over leadership of the independence movement. In 1931 he was elected to the State Council, serving for fifteen years as Minister of Agriculture and Lands. In 1943 the British government made the Whitehall Declaration on constitutional change, and Senanayake put forward a proposal for dominion status. In 1946 he resigned his ministry to press for full independence and formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-party">United National Party</a> by amalgamating three pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> parties.</p><p>After the 1947 elections he formed a government in coalition with the All Ceylon Tamil Congress and became the island's first prime minister on 24 September 1947; Ceylon marked its independence on 4 February 1948. His government's Gal Oya scheme relocated over 250,000 people, and the Ceylon Citizenship Act of 1948 left more than 700,000 people, about 11 per cent of the population, without citizenship, against fierce opposition in Parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On the morning of 21 March 1952 Senanayake was riding on Galle Face Green when his horse broke into a gallop and he fell from the saddle. He remained unconscious in a nursing home for thirty-two hours, was believed to have suffered a stroke, and died on 22 March 1952.</p><p>His son Dudley Senanayake succeeded him as prime minister. He is respected by Sinhalese and some Muslims and called the Father of the Nation, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> objected to his citizenship laws, which disenfranchised nearly all Tamils of recent Indian origin in the central highlands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “D. S. Senanayake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It gained independence peacefully in 1948 as the Dominion of Ceylon, with <b>D. S. Senanayake</b> as first prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-ceylon">British Ceylon</a></li><li><blockquote><b>D. S. Senanayake</b> of the United National Party became the first prime minister, and the Soulbury Constitution provided a Westminster-style parliament.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-ceylon">Dominion of Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mirigama, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=7.24,80.13&amp;year=1927" aria-label="Mirigama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of D. S. Senanayake"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4167 4382h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4167 4382h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=7.24,80.13&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
